(window.PF_CAT_SHARD=window.PF_CAT_SHARD||{})["76453"]={"2528":{"i":"2528","entryRequirements":"Academic Requirements: Applicants must have: Bachelor's Degree or a Graduate Certificate or Diploma in a similar subject or relevant skills and knowledge acquired through appropriate work or professional experience. English Language Requirements: If English is not the applicant's first language, applicants will need to provide evidence from: An International English Language Testing System (IELTS) overall academic score of at least 6.5 (with at least 6.0 in each band) from a single test issued within the last two years OR A test or qualification on the acceptable alternatives English Proficiency Outcomes Table OR Achieved NCEA Level 3 University Entrance requirements Alternative requirements: Applicants who are unable to evidence that they meet Academic entry requirements, but who can demonstrate acquired skills for tertiary study gained through study, work and/or life experience in a similar area, may be approved for alternative entry by the Curriculum Manager (or delegate)","strategicPurposeStatement":"The aim of the Postgraduate Diploma in Sustainable Aquaculture programme is to produce work-ready graduates with the personal attributes, advanced specialised knowledge and practical skills necessary to make an immediate and innovative contribution to sustainable aquaculture development whether locally, nationally or internationally.","graduateProfile":"Graduates will be able to reflect on the complex and dynamic technological, social and economic environments in which sustainable aquaculture operations are undertaken. They will have advanced technical knowledge and skills required to design and conduct independent research utilising recognised industry methodologies, rigorous data analysis and effective problem solving techniques. Graduates will have the ability to critically evaluate aquaculture systems in terms of commercial, technical, environmental, ethical and social sustainability and make recommendations for improvement. They will have demonstrated their ability to adapt and innovate in an industry which is rapidly changing and to operate knowledgeably, productively and professionally in management, technical and research and development roles.","educationPathway":"Further post graduate study.","developedBy":"Nelson Marlborough Institute of Technology (NMIT)","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=2528"},"3860":{"i":"3860","entryRequirements":"Academic Requirements \n\r\nA Bachelor's degree. \n\r\nAlternative Requirements \n\r\nApplicants unable to evidence that they meet Academic Entry requirements, but who can demonstrate acquired skills for tertiary study gained through study, work and/or life experience, may be approved for alternative entry. \n\r\nNote: Course requirements may be reduced where you have relevant credits from previous study, or evidence of similar learning or experience in the workplace. Contact Ara for advice. \n\r\nEnglish Requirements \n\r\nApplicants for whom English is not their first language may also be required to provide evidence of English language skills as listed below. Applicants who do not have evidence of English language skills and are a New Zealand citizen or permanent resident should contact Ara for advice. \n\r\n - IELTS 6.5 Academic (no lower than 6.0 in any subtest). For acceptable alternatives refer to the English Proficiency Outcomes Chart on the Ara website. \n\r\nNote: IELTS scores used must be taken from a single IELTS Test Report Form and are valid for two years from the date of the test. \n\r\nAdmission \n\r\nApplicants who meet the requirements for admission are accepted into the programme in the order their applications are received.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The aim of the Master of Sustainable Practice is to enable students to develop and apply advanced understandings of theories, driving forces, and worldviews, including Te Ao Maori, related to sustainable practice within the context of their discipline specific knowledge. The programme embraces principles of the Te Tiriti o Waitangi to facilitate collaboration and innovation and applies an interdisciplinary framework to address complex or wicked sustainability problems. Graduates will be able to advocate for, and lead change towards, more sustainable practices, apply high level research skills to plan and conduct independent inquiry projects related to sustainability in professional contexts, and will be independent learners who can utilise and undertake research to formulate solutions to sustainability issues. The Master of Sustainable Practice is aimed at people in professional practice contexts or new graduates looking for advanced study and research skills to become leaders of sustainable practice.","graduateProfile":"Graduates of this qualification will be able to:  \n\r\n1. Show evidence of advanced sustainability knowledge including sophisticated theoretical concepts, global perspectives and future thinking related to sustainable practice.  \n\r\n2. Engage in rigorous intellectual analysis and critically evaluate findings, discussions, and debates in literature, theory and practice pertaining to sustainable practice.  \n\r\n3. Enact cross cultural perspectives which embrace understanding and engagement with indigenous bodies of knowledge, practices and worldviews with a key focus on Te Ao Māori and bicultural Aotearoa New Zealand.  \n\r\n4. Articulate and apply a vision of sustainable practice which constructively takes account of multiple perspectives and reflexively recognises personal and professional values.  \n\r\n5. Apply sustainable practice skills and knowledge, including indigenous bodies of knowledge, to professional practice contexts in ways which innovatively and creatively engage with complex sustainability problems.  \n\r\n6. Utilise an interdisciplinary framework and work collaboratively in multidisciplinary teams to engage with complex sustainable practice problems.  \n\r\n7. Advocate for and implement change towards sustainability within professional practice contexts.  \n\r\n8. Work independently to demonstrate a high order of skill in the planning, execution and completion of research related to sustainability within professional practice contexts.","educationPathway":"Graduates of Master of Sustainable Practice qualifications may be able to enrol in doctoral studies, depending on the entry criteria of the institution concerne.d","developedBy":"Ara Institute of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=3860"},"3861":{"i":"3861","entryRequirements":"Academic Requirements \n\r\nA Bachelor's degree. \n\r\nAlternative Requirements \n\r\nApplicants unable to evidence that they meet Academic Entry requirements, but who can demonstrate acquired skills for tertiary study gained through study, work and/or life experience, may be approved for alternative entry. \n\r\nNote: Course requirements may be reduced where you have relevant credits from previous study, or evidence of similar learning or experience in the workplace. Contact Ara for advice. \n\r\nEnglish Requirements \n\r\nApplicants for whom English is not their first language may also be required to provide evidence of English language skills as listed below. Applicants who do not have evidence of English language skills and are a New Zealand citizen or permanent resident should contact Ara for advice. \n\r\n - IELTS 6.5 Academic (no lower than 6.0 in any subtest). For acceptable alternatives refer to the English Proficiency Outcomes Chart on the Ara website. \n\r\nNote: IELTS scores used must be taken from a single IELTS Test Report Form and are valid for two years from the date of the test. \n\r\nAdmission \n\r\nApplicants who meet the requirements for admission are accepted into the programme in the order their applications are received.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The aim of the Postgraduate Diploma in Sustainable Practice is to enable students to develop and apply advanced understandings of theories, driving forces, and worldviews, including Te Ao Maori, related to sustainable practice within the context of their discipline specific knowledge. The programme embraces principles of the Te Tiriti o Waitangi to facilitate collaboration and innovation and applies an interdisciplinary framework to address complex sustainability problems. Graduates will be able to engage in rigorous intellectual analysis, criticism and problem-solving, utilise research, and advocate for, and contribute to, more sustainable practices in a range of contexts. The PG Diploma in Sustainable Practice is aimed at people looking for more in-depth, longer duration professional development and advanced study to enable them to become agents for change related to sustainable practice.","graduateProfile":"Graduates of this qualification will be able to:  \n\r\n1. Show evidence of advanced sustainability knowledge including sophisticated theoretical concepts, global perspectives and future thinking related to sustainable practice.  \n\r\n2. Engage in rigorous intellectual analysis and critically evaluate findings, discussions, and debates in literature, theory and practice pertaining to sustainable practice.  \n\r\n3. Enact cross cultural perspectives which embrace understanding and engagement with indigenous bodies of knowledge, practices and worldviews with a key focus on Te Ao Māori and bicultural Aotearoa New Zealand.  \n\r\n4. Articulate and apply a vision of sustainable practice which constructively takes account of multiple perspectives and reflexively recognises personal and professional values.  \n\r\n5. Apply sustainable practice skills and knowledge, including indigenous bodies of knowledge, to professional practice contexts in ways which innovatively and creatively engage with complex sustainability problems.","educationPathway":"Graduates of the Postgraduate Diploma in Sustainable Practice may enrol in further Postgraduate qualification programmes.","developedBy":"Ara Institute of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=3861"},"3862":{"i":"3862","entryRequirements":"Academic Requirements \n\r\nA Bachelor's degree. \n\r\nAlternative Requirements \n\r\nApplicants unable to evidence that they meet Academic Entry requirements, but who can demonstrate acquired skills for tertiary study gained through study, work and/or life experience, may be approved for alternative entry. \n\r\nNote: Course requirements may be reduced where you have relevant credits from previous study, or evidence of similar learning or experience in the workplace. Contact Ara for advice. \n\r\nEnglish Requirements \n\r\nApplicants for whom English is not their first language may also be required to provide evidence of English language skills as listed below. Applicants who do not have evidence of English language skills and are a New Zealand citizen or permanent resident should contact Ara for advice. \n\r\n - IELTS 6.5 Academic (no lower than 6.0 in any subtest). For acceptable alternatives refer to the English Proficiency Outcomes Chart on the Ara website. \n\r\nNote: IELTS scores used must be taken from a single IELTS Test Report Form and are valid for two years from the date of the test. \n\r\nAdmission \n\r\nApplicants who meet the requirements for admission are accepted into the programme in the order their applications are received.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The aim of the Postgraduate Certificate in Sustainable Practice is to enable students to develop core, foundational knowledge about theories, driving forces, and worldviews, including Te Ao Maori, related to sustainable practice. The programme embraces principles of the Te Tiriti o Waitangi to facilitate application of such knowledge to specific discipline contexts whilst building the capability to recognise that interdisciplinary frameworks and research can help to address complex sustainability problems. The PG Certificate in Sustainable Practice is an ideal first step towards postgraduate study aimed at people with an interest in sustainable practice who wish to develop higher level understanding and specialist skills from their undergraduate degree.","graduateProfile":"Graduates of this qualification will be able to: \n\r\n1. Show evidence of advanced sustainability knowledge including sophisticated theoretical concepts, global perspectives and future thinking related to sustainable practice. \n\r\n2. Engage in rigorous intellectual analysis and critically evaluate findings, discussions, and debates in literature, theory and practice pertaining to sustainable practice. \n\r\n3. Enact cross cultural perspectives which embrace understanding and engagement with indigenous bodies of knowledge, practices and worldviews with a key focus on Te Ao Māori and bicultural Aotearoa New Zealand. \n\r\n4. Articulate and apply a vision of sustainable practice which constructively takes account of multiple perspectives and reflexively recognises personal and professional values.","educationPathway":"Graduates will be able to apply for further postgraduate study.","developedBy":"Ara Institute of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=3862"},"4248":{"i":"4248","entryRequirements":"The academic entry requirement for the Postgraduate Certificate and Postgraduate Diploma in Wine Business and Innovation is one of the following: \n\r\n* an NZQCF-listed undergraduate degree (or equivalent) or \n\r\n* at the Dean's discretion, where an applicant's situation is compelling but not covered by the requirements listed above.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The Postgraduate Certificate in Wine Business and Innovation is a Level 8 programme of 60 credits, which enables students who have completed a Bachelor's Degree in Business, Viticulture, Wine Science or similar, and/ or suitable work experience to achieve a postgraduate level qualification in the Wine Business and Innovation area.  \n\r\nThe programme can be completed in 6 months full-time or up to 4 years part-time, depending on the student's previous study and experience, and specific course preferences.","graduateProfile":"Graduates should exhibit critical thinking and problem solving  \n\r\n* think clearly, rationally, and independently when making management decisions  \n\r\n* diagnose and analyse business problems logically in order to produce optimal solutions  \n\r\nPlanning and change management skills  \n\r\n* contribute to the development and implementation of strategic and operational wine industry related plans  \n\r\nResearch and informational skills  \n\r\n* find, and use, information to extend research work and consolidate knowledge in industry practice  \n\r\nCommunication skills  \n\r\n* communicate management decisions and information both written and orally","educationPathway":"Further postgraduate study","developedBy":"Eastern Institute of Technology","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=4248"},"4380":{"i":"4380","entryRequirements":"The academic entry requirement for the Master of Wine Business and Innovation (240) is one of the following: * an NZQCF-listed undergraduate degree (or equivalent) in a related field such as Oenology, Viticulture, Business or Management; * at the Dean's discretion, where an applicant's situation is compelling but not covered by the requirements listed above. All applicants must demonstrate an acceptable level of English language fluency prior to acceptance in the programme. For international students, ways in which English language fluency may be demonstrated include the following: * successful study of a programme in which English was the language of instruction (subject to the conditions in EIT's Database for English Language Proficiency (DELP)) * completion of a New Zealand Certificate in English Language (Level 5 with any endorsement) * IELTS Academic score of 6.5, with no band score lower than 6, achieved in a single test within the two years preceding enrolment * accepted international equivalents to the above IELTS scores, achieved in a single test within the two years preceding enrolment.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The Master of Wine Business and Innovation (240) is a Level 9 programme of 240 credits at levels 8 and 9 with at least 45 credits at level 9, completed by coursework, or a combination of coursework and either a project or research. It enables students who have completed a Bachelor's Degree in business or related field to achieve a postgraduate level qualification in the Wine Business and Innovation area. The rationale for this two year programme is to enable students to succeed on a full Master's Degree and achieve at level 9 with a strong foundation at level 8.   \n\r\nThe programme can be completed in 24 months full-time or up to six years part-time, depending on the student's previous study and experience, and specific course preferences.","graduateProfile":"Graduates will be able to:   \n\r\n* apply independent rigorous intellectual analysis to management decisions   \n\r\n* critically and effectively analyze existing and emerging problems in order to produce optimal solutions   \n\r\nPlanning and change management skills   \n\r\n* work both independently and co-operatively to produce and implement strategic and operational business plans   \n\r\n* apply advanced knowledge to new situations and generate appropriate solutions   \n\r\n* adapt to emerging paradigms within the profession   \n\r\nResearch and informational skills   \n\r\n* critically evaluate findings and discussions in applied management literature   \n\r\n* apply business and/or management and /or technical research in the wine industry   \n\r\n* research, analyse, and argue from evidence   \n\r\nCommunication skills   \n\r\n* effectively communicate business decisions and information both in writing and orally   \n\r\n* communicate across cultures within a wine industry context.","educationPathway":"Possible further study in the area of wine business and innovation.","developedBy":"Eastern Institute of Technology","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=4380"},"4381":{"i":"4381","entryRequirements":"The academic entry requirement for the Master of Wine Business and Innovation (180) is one of the following:\n\r\n* an NZQCF-listed undergraduate degree (or equivalent) in a related field such as Oenology, Viticulture, Business or Management; with a minimum of the equivalent to a B- average (when aligned to the EIT-specified 11-point achievement-based grading system);\n\r\nor\n\r\n* a Bachelor Honours or Postgraduate degree in the Oenology, Viticulture, Business or Management or related fields with 120 credits at Level 8;\n\r\nor\n\r\n* a degree or equivalent professional qualification in a related field with a minimum of the equivalent to a B average (as per the EIT-specified 11-point achievement-based grading system) and evidence of relevant experience in the Viticulture, Oenology, Business or related fields at degree level, and assessment from the programme coordinator;\n\r\nor\n\r\n* at the Dean's discretion, where an applicant's situation is compelling but not covered by the requirements listed above.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The Master of Wine Business and Innovation (180) is a Level 9 programme of 180 credits at levels 8 and 9 with at least 45 credits at level 9, completed by coursework, or a combination of coursework and either a project or research. It enables students who have completed a Bachelor's Degree in Business, Viticulture or Wine Science or similar, to achieve a postgraduate level qualification in the Wine Business and Innovation area.   \n\r\nThe programme can be completed in 18 months full-time or up to six years part-time, depending on the student's previous study and experience, and specific course preferences.","graduateProfile":"Graduate Outcomes (Graduates will be able to:)   \n\r\n* apply independent rigorous intellectual analysis to management decisions   \n\r\n* critically and effectively analyze existing and emerging problems in order to produce optimal solutions   \n\r\nPlanning and change management skills   \n\r\n* work both independently and co-operatively to produce and implement strategic and operational business plans   \n\r\n* apply advanced knowledge to new situations and generate appropriate solutions   \n\r\n* adapt to emerging paradigms within the profession   \n\r\nResearch and informational skills   \n\r\n* critically evaluate findings and discussions in applied management literature   \n\r\n* apply business and/or management and /or technical research in the wine industry   \n\r\n* research, analyse, and argue from evidence   \n\r\nCommunication skills   \n\r\n* effectively communicate business decisions and information both in writing and orally   \n\r\n* communicate across cultures within a wine industry context.","educationPathway":"Further relevant study","developedBy":"Eastern Institute of Technology","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=4381"},"4382":{"i":"4382","entryRequirements":"The academic entry requirement for the Postgraduate Certificate and Postgraduate Diploma in Wine Business and Innovation is one of the following: * an NZQCF-listed undergraduate degree (or equivalent) or * at the Dean's discretion, where an applicant's situation is compelling but not covered by the requirements listed above. All applicants must demonstrate an acceptable level of English language fluency prior to acceptance in the programme. For international students, ways in which English language fluency may be demonstrated include the following: * successful study of a programme in which English was the language of instruction (subject to the conditions in EIT's Database for English Language Proficiency (DELP)) * completion of a New Zealand Certificate in English Language (Level 5 with any endorsement) * IELTS Academic score of 6.5, with no band score lower than 6, achieved in a single test within the two years preceding enrolment * accepted international equivalents to the above IELTS scores, achieved in a single test within the two years preceding enrolment.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The Postgraduate Diploma in Wine Business and Innovation is a Level 8 programme of 120 credits at levels 7 and 8, with at least 75 credits at level 8, which enables students who have completed a Bachelor's Degree in Business, Viticulture, Wine Science or similar and/ or suitable work experience to achieve a postgraduate level qualification in the Wine Business and Innovation area.   \n\r\nThe programme can be completed in 12 months full-time or up to four years part-time, depending on the student's previous study and experience, and specific course preferences. Students who initially enrolled in the Master's Degrees and are not in a position to complete it, can transfer applicable courses to the postgraduate diploma for the purposes of qualification completion.","graduateProfile":"Graduates will be able to:   \n\r\n* engage in rigorous intellectual analysis, criticism, and problem-solving in a wine industry context   \n\r\n* diagnose wine industry problems critically and logically in order to arrive at optimal solutions   \n\r\nPlanning and change management skills   \n\r\n* devise, and implement, strategic and operational plans   \n\r\n* apply technical and theoretical knowledge to manage and control complex situations and to generate new solutions   \n\r\nResearch and informational skills   \n\r\n* find and use information to extend research work and consolidate knowledge in wine industry practice   \n\r\n* apply business and/or management and /or technical research in industry   \n\r\nCommunication skills   \n\r\n* effectively communicate business decisions and information both written and orally   \n\r\n* demonstrate understanding of communication issues across cultures within a wine industry context.","educationPathway":"Master's degree","developedBy":"Eastern Institute of Technology","qualityAssuredBy":"New Zealand Qualifications Authority","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=4382"},"CN1155":{"i":"CN1155","strategicPurposeStatement":"Gratuates of the PGCertSFI will be able to demonstrate advanced and integrated understanding of disruptive strategies for sustainability, and transformation from social, environmental, business, Indigenous perspectives and challenges. Graduates will be able to synthesise sustainability research and professional literatures and effectively communicate to a range of audiences. They will be confident in collaborative problem solving and community engagement, guided by sustainability principles and ethical values, including how to collaborate in a Treaty frame with Mana Whenua and with Indigenous communities.","graduateProfile":"A range of assessment methods will be used including coursework, quizzes, short answer exercises, written assignments, project work, case studies, presentations and examinations.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN1155"},"LI7676":{"i":"LI7676","entryRequirements":"Students must qualify for the award of a degree of an accredited higher education institution in New Zealand approved for the purpose by the Academic Board; provided that the Academic Board may, at its discretion, require such a person to complete to its satisfaction a course of study prescribed for the purpose, either as a prerequisite to, or concurrently with, the course of study for the degree of Master of Sustainable Forest Management;  i. Entry to the programme will be limited to students with a prior undergraduate qualification at above average standard (normally a B average or better is to be obtained in the student's undergraduate preparation);  or (b) be admitted with equivalent status (ad eundem statum) as entitled to proceed to the degree of Master of Sustainable Tropical Forest Management.","strategicPurposeStatement":"This offshore programme provides professional, postgraduate-level education in sustainable forest management for managers in the Sarawak forest industry.","developedBy":"Lincoln University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=LI7676"},"CN064":{"i":"CN064","entryRequirements":"Candidates must have a degree, be approved by the Dean of Science and have satisfied the medical examination prescribed by Antarctica New Zealand.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have developed an overview of the role of Antarctica and the Southern Ocean in the understanding and resolution of global issues and have knowledge of contemporary research activity in the Antarctic and Southern Ocean.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN064"},"LI7575":{"i":"LI7575","entryRequirements":"Entry is by completion of an undergraduate degree relevant to environmental policy and management with a B average at 300 level.","strategicPurposeStatement":"MEPM graduates will demonstrate an advanced level of knowledge of the theoretical and conceptual underpinnings of environmental policy and management and its practice. Graduates will have an advanced level of understanding of the socio-cultural, economic, legal and institutional contexts in which environmental policy and management take place. Graduates will have intellectual, evaluation and communication skills which will allow them to critically, effectively and thoughtfully apply their knowledge to the development and application of environmental policy and approaches to management, contribute to debates and competently identify options for future directions.","developedBy":"Lincoln University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=LI7575"},"CN0806":{"i":"CN0806","entryRequirements":"Bachelor of Forestry Science or other appropriate bachelor's degree.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates can demonstrate advanced knowledge of a field of forestry science; apply and communicate effectively forestry principles and concepts; understand, evaluate, access and review critically, new information (including findings and discussions in the literature); engage in rigorous intellectual analysis, criticism and problem solving; work independently; carry out research, write a composite report and communicate effectively concepts, results, and conclusions. Completion of this degree can lead to professional employment or the possibility of doctoral study.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN0806"},"LI7640":{"i":"LI7640","entryRequirements":"An approved Bachelor's degree","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will be able to:  ? Apply of the principles of water science and management. ? Critique knowledge and skills related to the concepts, data collection, analysis, use and ownership of data and problem-solving techniques for water science and management. ? Apply frameworks used for decision making in water management, and the cultural, Te Tiriti o Waitangi, ethical, economic, and societal factors that impact decision making. ? Work as a constructive team member.  ? Use effective communication strategies.  ? Demonstrate biculturalism in the context of water science and management. ? Prepared for employment as water scientists and decision makers in the public and private sectors within and beyond New Zealand. ? Able to develop integrated projects.","developedBy":"Lincoln University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=LI7640"},"MY1092":{"i":"MY1092","entryRequirements":"A relevant bachelor's degree.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Holders will have gained advanced knowledge of and skills in planning and will be prepared for employment as planners or for further postgraduate study.","developedBy":"Massey University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=MY1092"},"LI7620":{"i":"LI7620","entryRequirements":"A relevant bachelors degree with appropriate grades.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates of this programme will have the skills and knowledge required to work in the international arena of nature conservation or to have an international perspective while employed locally.","developedBy":"Lincoln University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=LI7620"},"MY0244":{"i":"MY0244","entryRequirements":"Appropriate degree or equivalent.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will demonstrate self-directed and independent learning, and will be able to communicate effectively. They will demonstrate an understanding of the environment and of society's impact upon the environment. They will reflect objectively on problems and opportunities, think creatively, employ analytical skills, and develop and present clear responses to current environmental situations. They will demonstrate technical competence in assessing environmental issues and develop relevant and appropriate environmental management strategies. They will identify, evaluate, document, disseminate and employ knowledge in the context of environmental management. Graduates will have advanced skills and knowledge in specialised environmentally oriented subjects.","developedBy":"Massey University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=MY0244"},"VI00B3":{"i":"VI00B3","entryRequirements":"Bachelor's degree in a relevant subject","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will be able to demonstrate knowledge of and research in environmental issues and management at an advanced level.","developedBy":"Victoria University of Wellington","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=VI00B3"},"LI7666":{"i":"LI7666","entryRequirements":"Brief entry requirements\tQualify for the award of an undergraduate degree in a related discipline.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates of this programme will be able to successfully develop careers in the Biological Heritage, Biosecurity and Bio-Protection workforces.","graduateProfile":"The Master of Bioprotection is a 180-credit qualification normally taught over three semesters of full-time study. There is a choice of two streams, one focusing on Vertebrate Pest Managements and the other on Plant Pests Management. Each stream has three core papers, with the remaining six papers chosen from common core and specialisation-specific courses.","developedBy":"Lincoln University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=LI7666"},"LI0803":{"i":"LI0803","entryRequirements":"A relevant undergraduate degree with an average of B+ or higher in 300 level subjects.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates of the BEM(Hons) will have the knowledge and skills necessary to contribute to the study, assessment and sustainable management of environmental and resource issues. Graduates will have an understanding of the biophysical sciences, economics and social sciences required to meet triple bottom line objectives.","developedBy":"Lincoln University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=LI0803"},"CN0207":{"i":"CN0207","entryRequirements":"An approved Bachelor's degree.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will be knowledgeable and skilled in understanding some of the key challenges cities face, and be aware of how cities can respond to these challenges. They will have an appreciation of the range of contexts, processes and actors that constitute urban environments. They will have the appropriate attitudes, skills and knowledge to be employed professionally in any organisation involved in urban resilience and renewal; some of the most important skills acquired will be the ability to learn independently, and to communicate effectively.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN0207"},"AU0415":{"i":"AU0415","entryRequirements":"Bachelor of Planning or an equivalent qualification","strategicPurposeStatement":"MPlan graduates will demonstrate an advanced level of understanding of the practical and theoretical underpinnings of this discipline and will be able to engage in research and debate on current issues in planning.","developedBy":"University of Auckland","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AU0415"},"LI0682":{"i":"LI0682","entryRequirements":"A relevant bachelor's degree with an average of B+ or higher in final-year papers.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates from the BV&O(Hons) will have the broad range of skills and aptitudes required for success in the field of viticulture and oenology. The programme provides a pathway for students who want to progress to masters or doctoral level study in the field.","developedBy":"Lincoln University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=LI0682"},"CN1154":{"i":"CN1154","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have a robust foundation of analytical tools and capabilities to contribute meaningfully to sustainable change within diverse sectors. They will have the capabilities to critically evaluate complex ethical, social and environmental justice issues that impact sustainability decision making and application of disruptive strategies in diverse contexts and communities. They will be confident in communicating and devising strategies for sustainability transformation in their chosen field of practice.","graduateProfile":"A range of assessment methods will be used including coursework, quizzes, short answer exercises, written assignments, project work, case studies, presentations and examinations.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN1154"},"LI4570":{"i":"LI4570","entryRequirements":"A New Zealand entrance qualification or equivalent.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates of the BEPP (Hons) will develop a sound knowledge of both scholarship and practice of the strategic importance to environment policy and planning, within statutory, institutional and organisational frameworks.","developedBy":"Lincoln University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=LI4570"},"OO0498":{"i":"OO0498","entryRequirements":"A bachelor's degree with an average grade of at least B+ in the final year of study, or equivalent.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates of this degree programme have an understanding of the processes and interactions at work in ecological communities, and will recognise the principles of wildlife population persistence, change, or decline. Students will graduate with a toolkit of techniques with which to gather and analyse information and answer questions about wildlife populations, and will be able to apply their skills to address real-life problems. Above all, graduates will have an appreciation of the need for and an ability to apply critical thinking, scientific rigour, and a systematic approach to the management of wildlife.","developedBy":"University of Otago","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=OO0498"},"LI0035":{"i":"LI0035","entryRequirements":"A New Zealand University entrance qualification or equivalent.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates of the BAgrSc(Hons) will be able to apply scientific concepts and professional skills, including practical skills, to all aspects of agriculture. They will be able to evaluate and assess the implications of change on primary production systems and apply knowledge and skills in technology management to implement technology transfer.","developedBy":"Lincoln University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=LI0035"},"LI7065":{"i":"LI7065","entryRequirements":"An undergraduate qualification in a related discipline with at least a B grade average in the final year of their undergraduate qualification. Typically students will have a background in physical and biological science; some knowledge of the wine industry would be considered as advantageous.","strategicPurposeStatement":"To provide graduates with the knowledge and skills to successfully develop careers in the grape-growing and wine-making industries.","developedBy":"Lincoln University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=LI7065"},"MY0423":{"i":"MY0423","entryRequirements":"A relevant bachelors degree.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have advanced knowledge and skills in resource and environmental planning theory and techniques.","developedBy":"Massey University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=MY0423"},"LI0375":{"i":"LI0375","entryRequirements":"A relevant bachelors degree with appropriate grades.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have advanced skills and knowledge in agricultural science through postgraduate coursework and research.","developedBy":"Lincoln University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=LI0375"},"LI7577":{"i":"LI7577","entryRequirements":"Relevance and standard of undergraduate studies and experience will be the criteria for approval. Entry to the programme will be limited to students with a prior undergraduate qualification in an appropriate discipline at above average standard (normally a B average or better is to be obtained in the student's undergraduate preparation).","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will be qualified to contribute to transforming the agricultural sector, to make agrifood production more environmentally sustainable and in a way that better accommodates different worldviews. The programme provides an employable combination of analytical and research skills, matauraka and agricultural science knowledge, couched within knowledge in the use of policy frameworks.","graduateProfile":"All courses will be assessed using a range of tests, assignments, experimental reports, individual and/or group presentations.","developedBy":"Lincoln University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=LI7577"},"CN1156":{"i":"CN1156","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates of the Master of Sustainable Futures and Innovation will have advanced knowledge and applied skills, including Indigenous knowledge/matauranga Māori, to interrogate the complex systems and processes that drive (un)sustainability and to reshape dynamics in business, policy, and international agencies for regenerative futures. They will be able to critically engage in transdisciplinary, collaborative problem solving to identify and apply strategies for disruptive innovative and regenerative change. Graduates will also understand ethical, social and environmental justice issues that impact decision making, alongside gaining skills for collaboration and community engagement in a Treaty framework. Graduates will be multi-solvers, competent in applying problem solving and communication skills to research, analyse, evaluate and present arguments for sustainable futures to a range of audiences.","graduateProfile":"A range of assessment will be used including coursework, quizzes, short answer exercises, written assignments, project work, case studies, presentations and examinations.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN1156"},"VI00R6":{"i":"VI00R6","entryRequirements":"A Bachelor's degree in a relevant subject","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will be able to demonstrate knowledge of environmental issues and management at an advanced level.","developedBy":"Victoria University of Wellington","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=VI00R6"},"MY0427":{"i":"MY0427","entryRequirements":"Bachelor of Engineering with Honours, Bachelor of Science with Honours, or Bachelor of Technology with Honours degree or equivalent.","strategicPurposeStatement":"MDairyScTech graduates will have intellectual independence and technical leadership well above the bachelor level and will be able to critically evaluate information and communicate effectively. Only students who have approved employment in the New Zealand dairy industry have entry to this programme and upon completion they will have good quantitative skills, be able to solve a range of both theoretical and practical problems, have acquired the ability to work independently and with groups.","developedBy":"Massey University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=MY0427"},"AU5747":{"i":"AU5747","entryRequirements":"Completed the requirements for a Bachelor of Science with a minimum GPA or completed the requirements for a relevant Bachelors degree and passed 60 points in the Postgraduate Diploma in Science in Environmental Management with a minimum GPA.","strategicPurposeStatement":"MEnvMgt graduates will receive advanced training in environmental management and will undertake research in this field.  On completion of the degree, students will be equipped to apply their skills and understanding in careers in environmental management, in an ethically informed manner.","developedBy":"University of Auckland","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AU5747"},"MY1091":{"i":"MY1091","entryRequirements":"A relevant bachelors degree.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Holders will have advanced knowledge and skills in planning.","developedBy":"Massey University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=MY1091"},"LI0399":{"i":"LI0399","entryRequirements":"A relevant bachelors degree with appropriate grades.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have advanced skills and knowledge in horticultural science through postgraduate coursework and research.","developedBy":"Lincoln University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=LI0399"},"LI6609":{"i":"LI6609","entryRequirements":"A New Zealand Bachelor's Degree in a relevant field or equivalent.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The outcome of this qualification is to outfit students with the skills they need to contribute effectively to sustainable management of freshwater resources in New Zealand and internationally. Graduates of this programme should be well positioned to provide to innovative solutions to water management problems, and to coordinate, adapt and manage water use, allocation, monitoring, protection and planning processes. Employment prospects include local, regional and national government, consultancies, industry and research organization.","developedBy":"Lincoln University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=LI6609"},"LI0182":{"i":"LI0182","entryRequirements":"A relevant bachelors degree.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates of this programme will be prepared for the challenging roles they will play in horticulture, both in New Zealand and other countries.","developedBy":"Lincoln University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=LI0182"},"LI7665":{"i":"LI7665","entryRequirements":"Students must have an undergraduate degree in a related discipline.  Students will be expected to have achieved at least a 'B' average in the final year of their undergraduate qualification.","strategicPurposeStatement":"To provide graduates with the knowledge and skills to successfully develop careers in the Biological Heritage, Biosecurity and Bio-Protection workforces.","developedBy":"Lincoln University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=LI7665"},"CN0804":{"i":"CN0804","entryRequirements":"Bachelor's degree in Forestry or a related study in New Zealand; or bachelor's degree and relevant experience in a forestry related area.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have gained advanced theoretical and practical knowledge of forestry science and can apply and communicate effectively forestry principles and concepts. They will understand, evaluate and review new information critically; engage in rigorous intellectual analysis, criticism and problem solving; work independently; carry out research, and communicate effectively concepts, results, and conclusions. Completion of this degree can lead to professional employment or the possibility of enrolling in the Master of Forest Science.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN0804"},"CN0242":{"i":"CN0242","entryRequirements":"A New Zealand university entrance qualification or equivalent.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will be able to demonstrate knowledge, matauranga Maori and understanding of the natural processes and anthropogenic impacts that contribute to environmental change. They will be able to demonstrate knowledge and skills related to the concepts, data collection, analysis methods, use and ownership of data and problem solving techniques for Environmental Science. They will be able to demonstrate an understanding of frameworks used for environmental decision-making and the cultural, Treaty, ethical, economic and societal factors that impact on decision making and have effective teamwork and communication skills. They will have an understanding of biculturalism in the context of Environmental Science and have completed 400 hours of work integrated learning. They will have undertaken research and produced an honours dissertation. They will be prepared for employment as Environmental Scientists in the public and private sectors within and beyond New Zealand.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN0242"},"VI0097":{"i":"VI0097","entryRequirements":"Bachelor's degree with First or Second Class Honours or extensive experience.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates can move into a range of disciplines or occupations, having advanced their understanding of policy issues and resource management implications of development work.","developedBy":"Victoria University of Wellington","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=VI0097"},"LIO595":{"i":"LIO595","entryRequirements":"Entry is by completion of an undergraduate degree relevant to environmental management with a B average at 300 level.","graduateProfile":"Graduates of the Postgraduate Certificate in Environmental Management programme will have advanced knowledge in the theory and practice of environmental management. They will be able to synthesise, and critically evaluate, a wide range of academic research of relevance to environmental management theory and practice, and communicate their findings effectively.","developedBy":"Lincoln University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=LIO595"},"LI6676":{"i":"LI6676","entryRequirements":"Entry requirements  This is a postgraduate qualification from Lincoln University. Normal entry is based on a Bachelor's degree with a B average from a recognised university. Forest industry experience and or other qualifications may also be considered as alternative entry criteria. Lectures and assessment will be in English and students must meet Lincoln University English language requirements","strategicPurposeStatement":"This offshore programme provides professional, postgraduate-level education in sustainable forest management for managers in the Sarawak forest industry.","developedBy":"Lincoln University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=LI6676"},"VI003M":{"i":"VI003M","entryRequirements":"A bachelor's degree in a relevant subject with at least a B grade average in the relevant courses.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will be able to demonstrate high-level understanding of the interconnected aspects of climate change science and policy, including physical causes, impacts, response measures and strategies in adaptation and mitigation, at local/national/international levels. They will be able to access, critique and apply relevant climate change scientific or policy research methods and evidence to scientific and policy questions, and apply knowledge about communication and behaviour change across cultures particularly with Maori and Pasifika communities. Graduates will be independent thinkers, able to continuously update their understanding and practice in the light of emerging knowledge, and critically reflect on their own knowledge framing. They will understand and demonstrate intellectual integrity and the ethics of scholarship and policy advice, and be active and engaged global citizens. Graduates will be well prepared to take on analytical and leadership roles in relevant science or policy advice sectors, and some may continue on to doctoral study.","developedBy":"Victoria University of Wellington","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=VI003M"},"AU4030":{"i":"AU4030","entryRequirements":"A Bachelor of Property or approved equivalent qualification.  Students who have completed a BProp(Hons) or a PGDipProp or an approved equivalent may gain admission to the second (thesis) year and take 120 credits to complete this programme.","strategicPurposeStatement":"MProp graduates will demonstrate an advanced understanding of the theory and application of property, be able to conduct appropriate research and critically analyse information.","developedBy":"University of Auckland","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AU4030"},"OO0021":{"i":"OO0021","entryRequirements":"Bachelor of Science degree in Zoology, Biology or Ecology or equivalent","strategicPurposeStatement":"This diploma provides the skills needed for employment in wildlife or ecological management, or research. Recipients are capable of self-directed learning, and can proceed to further postgraduate study.","developedBy":"University of Otago","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=OO0021"},"CN0166":{"i":"CN0166","entryRequirements":"A Bachelor's degree in an area related to Water Resource Management.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The outcome of this qualification is to outfit students with the skills they need to contribute effectively to sustainable management of freshwater resources in New Zealand and internationally. Graduates will be able to effectively apply their own specialist skills (eg in aspects of science, engineering, law) to freshwater resources management. They will be able to evaluate and justify the importance of practical experience, field work and scientific rigour in characterisation of a catchment system and assessing the effects of water use.","graduateProfile":"Not provided.","educationPathway":"Not provided.","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN0166"},"MY0345":{"i":"MY0345","entryRequirements":"Appropriate degree or equivalent.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have the capacity to acquire, analyse and interpret information pertaining to the physical, economic and environmental performance of land-based systems. They will have the ability to work independently, accept responsibility for personal development and achieve the academic standards and aptitude necessary for postgraduate study. They will have knowledge of scientific principles, industry structures, practices and current issues in agriculture, horticulture or the equine industry, and the role of new technologies. Graduates will be equipped with research methods skills based on topics that include the scientific method, systems approaches to problem-solving, survey methods, analysis and presentation of research results. They will be equipped with the research skills to implement projects and programmes and write theses. They will be able to produce a body of independent research and publish results of their findings.","developedBy":"Massey University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=MY0345"},"LI0146":{"i":"LI0146","entryRequirements":"A relevant bachelors degree.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates of this programme will be prepared for the challenging roles they will play in agriculture, both in New Zealand and other countries.","developedBy":"Lincoln University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=LI0146"},"OO0015":{"i":"OO0015","entryRequirements":"A bachelor's degree or equivalent.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates of this degree programme will have advanced knowledge of the process of formulating, implementing and evaluating environmental policies, plans and projects.","developedBy":"University of Otago","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=OO0015"},"LI0194":{"i":"LI0194","entryRequirements":"Entry is by completion of an undergraduate degree relevant to environmental management with a B average at 300 level.","graduateProfile":"Graduates of the Postgraduate Diploma in Environmental Management programme will have a coherent understanding of the theory and practice of environmental management. They will have a high level of critical analysis, evaluation, problem-solving and communication skills that are relevant to the many fields of environmental management","developedBy":"Lincoln University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=LI0194"},"MY0444":{"i":"MY0444","entryRequirements":"A Bachelors degree in Agriscience, or equivalent and a B grade average in 300 level majoring papers in the qualification considered for admission, or equivalent.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will be equipped with advanced skills and knowledge required of people in senior or leadership roles in the primary industries. This includes the role of science and technology, sustainable management and economics in resolving issues facing the primary industries. Importantly, graduates will have the ability to communicate the results of investigation and analysis of researchable problems, opportunities and issues.","developedBy":"Massey University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=MY0444"},"AU5601":{"i":"AU5601","entryRequirements":"A BSc with a GPA  <5 in 75 points above Stage II in Environmental Science, or equivalent and any prerequisited for the courses in the chosen subjects.","strategicPurposeStatement":"MEnvSci graduates will receive training in environmental science and research experience in this field. They will be equipped to apply their skills and understanding in careers in the environmental sciences, in an ethically informed manner.","developedBy":"University of Auckland","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AU5601"},"LI7630":{"i":"LI7630","entryRequirements":"A undergraduate qualification with the equivalent of a B+ average in the final year of undergraduate study in a related discipline.","strategicPurposeStatement":"This qualification will provide technical leaders for the agriculture and food and fibre industries and large agricultural or food-related organisations including processors and wholesalers, by training postgraduates who will become managers, data-driven scientists, consultants, farmers, sustainable production managers and strategic managers.","developedBy":"Lincoln University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=LI7630"},"CN0266":{"i":"CN0266","entryRequirements":"Applicants need to have qualified for the Postgraduate Diploma in Water Resource Management or have a Bachelor's degree in an equivalent area.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have the specialist skills they need to contribute effectively to sustainable managment of freshwater resources in New Zealand and internationally. They should be well positioned to provide innovative solutions to water management problems and to coordinate, adapt and manage water use, allocation, monitoring, protection and planning processes.","graduateProfile":"Not provided","educationPathway":"Not provided","developedBy":"University of Canterbury","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=CN0266"},"LI0250":{"i":"LI0250","entryRequirements":"A relevant bachelors degree with appropriate grades","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have advanced skills and knowledge in environmental/ resource policy through postgraduate coursework and research.","developedBy":"Lincoln University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=LI0250"},"LI6677":{"i":"LI6677","entryRequirements":"This is a postgraduate qualification from Lincoln University. Normal entry is based on a Bachelor's degree with a B average from a recognised university. Forest industry experience and or other qualifications may also be considered as alternative entry criteria. Lectures and assessment will be in English and students must meet Lincoln University English language requirements.","strategicPurposeStatement":"This offshore programme provides professional, postgraduate-level education in sustainable forest management for managers in the Sarawak forest industry.","developedBy":"Lincoln University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=LI6677"},"LI6675":{"i":"LI6675","entryRequirements":"This is a postgraduate qualification from Lincoln University. Normal entry is based on a Bachelor's degree with a B average from a recognised university. Forest industry experience and or other qualifications may also be considered as alternative entry criteria. Lectures and assessment will be in English and students must meet Lincoln University English language requirements","strategicPurposeStatement":"This offshore programme provides professional, postgraduate-level education in sustainable forest management for managers in the Sarawak forest industry.","developedBy":"Lincoln University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=LI6675"},"AU5782":{"i":"AU5782","entryRequirements":"A relevant qualification and offer of PhD conditional on completion of this PGCert.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will be able to apply highly specialised knowledge within the property discipline and demonstrate an advanced awareness and understanding in a global context. They will be able to analyse complex issues, evaluate relevant literature, develop scholarly arguments and communicate effectively with diverse audiences.?","graduateProfile":"Assessment modes include projects, presentations, reports and assignments.","developedBy":"University of Auckland","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=AU5782"},"LI7608":{"i":"LI7608","entryRequirements":"A New Zealand Bachelor's Degree in a relevant field or equivalent.","strategicPurposeStatement":"The outcome of this qualification is to outfit students with the specialist skills they need to contribute effectively to sustainable management of freshwater resources in New Zealand and internationally. Graduates of this programme should be well positioned to provide innovative solutions to water management problems, and to coordinate, adapt and manage water use, allocation, monitoring, protection and planning processes. Employment prospects include local, regional and national government, consultancies, industry and research organization.","developedBy":"Lincoln University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=LI7608"},"MY0343":{"i":"MY0343","entryRequirements":"Appropriate degree or equivalent.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will demonstrate self-directed and independent learning, and will be able to communicate effectively. They will demonstrate an understanding of the environment and of societys impact upon the environment. They will reflect objectively on problems and opportunities, think creatively, employ analytical skills, and develop and present clear responses to current environmental situations. They will demonstrate technical competence in assessing environmental issues and develop relevant and appropriate environmental management strategies. They will identify, evaluate, document, disseminate and employ knowledge in the context of environmental management. Graduates will have advanced skills and knowledge in specialised environmentally oriented subjects. They will be able to produce a body of independent research and publish results of their findings.","developedBy":"Massey University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=MY0343"},"LI0311":{"i":"LI0311","entryRequirements":"A relevant bachelors degree  with appropriate grades.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates of this programme will gain an in-depth understanding of resource/environmental management and sustainable development, at the local, national and global level.","developedBy":"Lincoln University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=LI0311"},"MY0221":{"i":"MY0221","entryRequirements":"A relevant bachelor's degree or equivalent.","strategicPurposeStatement":"Graduates will have an interdisciplinary perspective of the development of Maori resources, especially in relation to land-based industries.","developedBy":"Massey University","qualityAssuredBy":"Committee on University Academic Programmes","nzqaLink":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/nzqf/search/viewQualification.do?selectedItemKey=MY0221"}};
